% ============================================================================
% insulin-glucagon-trigger — the blood-glucose condition that triggers each
% of the pancreas's two glucose-regulating hormones, as the SAME NCI SEER
% spans also state (adj-facts-stdlib, BIOLOGY).
% ============================================================================
% A sibling to the already-shipped `hormone-glands.adj`
% (`hormone_gland(hormone, gland)`, WHICH gland secretes a hormone — insulin
% → pancreas, glucagon → pancreas, etc.). That table already ships, verbatim,
% as the `source` on each of those two rows, the trigger condition for the
% pancreas's two glucose hormones, and buried in those two spans, alongside
% the gland the parent table already captures, is a second fact the
% hormone→gland schema had no room for: WHAT blood-glucose condition
% triggers each hormone's release.
%
%   insulin → high
%     "Beta cells in the pancreatic islets secrete the hormone insulin in
%      response to a high concentration of glucose in the blood."
%
%   glucagon → low
%     "Alpha cells in the pancreatic islets secrete the hormone glucagons in
%      response to a low concentration of glucose in the blood."
%     (the page spells it "glucagons"; the hormone is glucagon)
%
% Recall is a binding query:
%
%     ? secretion_trigger(insulin, $Level)   % high
%
% This is a native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5, an ordinary two-column EXACT
% lookup): each `row` lowers to a ground relation `secretion_trigger(hormone,
% blood_glucose_level)` carrying THAT ROW'S OWN citation, with the
% envelope's locator and trust inherited (RS-5e, #14986), so the lookup
% above is the ordinary SLD binding query — the engine returns the
% trigger condition WITH its source, on the CPU, with zero model calls.
% Only these two hormones among `hormone-glands.adj`'s twelve have a
% blood-glucose trigger stated in this cited span, so this sibling is
% HONESTLY NARROW: it grounds exactly the pancreas's two glucose-regulating
% hormones and abstains on every other hormone (thyroxine, cortisol, etc.),
% which the source never ties to a blood-glucose trigger at all. The query
% also runs BACKWARD — bind a level and recall which hormone responds to it:
%
%     ? secretion_trigger($Hormone, low)
%       % glucagon
%
% Provenance: EACH ROW CARRIES ITS OWN span as its `source` (RS-5e,
% #14986); the locator and trust are inherited from the envelope
% field-by-field. Until this change the envelope WAS the insulin sentence
% and the glucagon sentence rode as a table-level `cites`, so a `glucagon`
% recall was warranted primarily by a sentence about INSULIN, and its own
% sentence reached no answer. Relocating it empties the corroborations
% array on every answer.
%
% The table-level `source` is now the page's framing sentence, "The pancreas
% is a long, soft organ that lies transversely along the posterior abdominal
% wall, posterior to the stomach, and extends from the region of the duodenum
% to the spleen.", which names NO hormone and NO cell type. Naming neither
% ROW KEY is not enough for a frame: this page's very next sentence frames
% the EXOCRINE pancreas ("This gland has an exocrine portion that secretes
% digestive enzymes that are carried through a duct to the duodenum."), and
% it too names no hormone -- yet it would frame the wrong half of the organ,
% since this table is entirely about the ENDOCRINE islets. A test arm
% forbids the page's whole hormone and cell-type taxonomy AND requires the
% frame to name the organ: forbidding alone would admit this page's own
% left-nav strings, which name no hormone and frame nothing at all.
%
% THE PLURAL IS THE PAGE'S. The glucagon span reads "the hormone glucagons".
% Measured on the fetched page: it writes "glucagons" in both of its prose
% sentences. That ships VERBATIM, under the #15320 precedent where the
% `balance` row quoted its page's own typo rather than a repaired form --
% correcting it to "glucagon" would cite a sentence this page does not
% contain, which is the defect this batch exists to remove.
%
% Both spans and the envelope were re-measured on the cited page: each occurs
% exactly once, and zero times against a real 404 control on the same host.
% The page's 41,705 bytes are pure ASCII -- zero above 127 -- and none of
% U+00A0, U+2013 or U+2019 appears in it. It DOES encode U+2014 three times
% as `&mdash;`, in the <title>, the <h1> and the suggested-citation block,
% so "pure ASCII" is a claim about the page's MARKUP, not about its decoded
% text; none of the three shipped spans touches any of the three. There is
% no invisible-byte hazard in what this table quotes, checked rather than
% assumed -- because two of the four conversions already merged in this
% batch DO ship non-ASCII on an evidence line: 03590 (`sun-layer`) has an en
% dash on two row sources and a right single quote sharing one of them, and
% 03570 (`mixture-types`) has a NON-BREAKING SPACE in its envelope, the
% worse of the two for being invisible. 03580 (`blood-cell-types`) and 03600
% (`muscle-nuclei-count`) are pure ASCII on those lines. Two of four, counted
% across the batch rather than inferred from the one I happened to remember.
%
% ONE NARROWING. "Only these two hormones ... have a blood-glucose trigger
% stated in this cited span", above, is scoped to the two sentences this
% table quotes. It is not a claim about the PAGE, which also says "The
% endocrine portion consists of the pancreatic islets, which secrete
% glucagons and insulin." -- naming both hormones while stating no trigger
% at all.
%
% This library carries the SAME two NCI SEER Training Modules spans that
% `hormone-glands.adj` already ships as its own per-row `source` values --
% that table is RS-5e too -- decoding with a different schema the
% trigger-condition half of each. This paragraph used to say the
% spans were reproduced "byte-for-byte" with "no new WebFetch"; that is
% retired, because the spans ARE now measured against the page itself (see
% the re-measurement above) -- the same retirement the sibling
% `muscle-striation.adj` already made. `trust authoritative` — the same tier
% `hormone-glands.adj` already carries for this pair (U.S. National Cancer
% Institute, a primary U.S. government teaching resource).
% (See ../README.md; feedback_nothing_human_authored,
% feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

table secretion_trigger {
    columns hormone, blood_glucose_level

    row (insulin, high) {
        source "Beta cells in the pancreatic islets secrete the hormone insulin in response to a high concentration of glucose in the blood."
    }
    row (glucagon, low) {
        source "Alpha cells in the pancreatic islets secrete the hormone glucagons in response to a low concentration of glucose in the blood."
    }

    source "The pancreas is a long, soft organ that lies transversely along the posterior abdominal wall, posterior to the stomach, and extends from the region of the duodenum to the spleen."
    locator "https://training.seer.cancer.gov/anatomy/endocrine/glands/pancreas.html"
    trust authoritative
}
